0}+1, y^{11}-1) = \\gcd(y^{10}+1, y+1)$.\n\nNow $y^{10}+1 \\mod (y+1)$. Since $y \\equiv -1 \\pmod{y+1}$, $y^{10} \\equiv (-1)^{10} = 1 \\pmod{y+1}$. So $y^{10}+1 \\equiv 2 \\pmod{y+1}$.\n\nThus $d = \\gcd(y^{10}+1, y+1) = \\gcd(2, y+1)$.\n\nNow $y = 51^{61}$. $51$ is odd, so $51^{61}$ is odd, so $y+1$ is even. Thus $d = 2$.\n\nNow $x = \\gcd(d^{671}+1, d^{610}-1) = \\gcd(2^{671}+1, 2^{610}-1)$.\n\nLet me compute this. Let $a = 2^{671}+1$ and $b = 2^{610}-1$.\n\n$\\gcd(2^{671}+1, 2^{610}-1)$.\n\nNote $2^{671} = 2^{61} \\cdot 2^{610}$. So $2^{671}+1 = 2^{61} \\cdot 2^{610} + 1 = 2^{61}(2^{610}-1) + 2^{61} + 1$.\n\nSo $\\gcd(2^{671}+1, 2^{610}-1) = \\gcd(2^{61}+1, 2^{610}-1)$.\n\nNow $2^{610} = (2^{61})^{10}$. Let $z = 2^{61}$. Then $\\gcd(z+1, z^{10}-1)$.\n\n$z^{10}-1 \\mod (z+1)$: $z \\equiv -1$, so $z^{10} \\equiv 1$, $z^{10}-1 \\equiv 0 \\pmod{z+1}$.\n\nSo $z+1 \\mid z^{10}-1$. Thus $\\gcd(z+1, z^{10}-1) = z+1 = 2^{61}+1$.\n\nSo $x = 2^{61}+1$.\n\nNow $X = x \\bmod 10 = (2^{61}+1) \\bmod 10$.\n\n$2^1 = 2, 2^2 = 4, 2^3 = 8, 2^4 = 16 \\equiv 6, 2^5 \\equiv 2 \\pmod{10}$. Cycle of length 4: 2, 4, 8, 6.\n\n$61 = 4 \\times 15 + 1$, so $2^{61} \\equiv 2^1 = 2 \\pmod{10}$.\n\nThus $x \\equiv 2+1 = 3 \\pmod{10}$.\n\n$X = 3$.\n\nLet me double-check the step where the problem says \"simplify $d$ to $d = (y^{10}+1, y+1)$\". This matches what I derived. Good.\n\nLet me verify $d=2$ once more. $y = 51^{61}$, odd, $y+1$ even, $y^{10}+1$: $y$ odd so $y^{10}$ odd, $y^{10}+1$ even. $\\gcd = \\gcd(2, y+1)$. Since $y+1$ is even, $\\gcd(2, y+1) = 2$. Yes, $d=2$.\n\nAnd $x = \\gcd(2^{671}+1, 2^{610}-1) = 2^{61}+1$, and $X = (2^{61}+1) \\bmod 10 = 3$.", "tool_calls": [], "created_at": 1786766118}
